{"description":"Compare file-level vs symbol+import code context (E2, Finding 6): does symbol slice + 1-hop import/caller (tree-sitter derived graph, bounded 32/64) reduce tokens and improve precision over dumping entire file bodies for the same 5 top grep hits. Provides evidence for the proposed Code Graph as derived Runtime cache (not CKO) and the file→symbol migration path.","investigationNotes":"Context: Finding 6 proposes minimum useful code context = symbol signature+40-80 line slice + 1-hop deps (imports + callee/caller name match) + lazy tests/config, not files; claims 60-80% reduction and higher reasoning quality. Currently no code graph exists — code accessed via raw read/grep (entire files). Need to know ROI before building eka-core/codegraph + code_context tool.\n\nExperiment E2 (also informs E7): Variant A = agent gets full file bodies for 5 top grep hits (baseline today); Variant B = agent gets symbol slices + 1-hop imports for same 5 symbols, full fetch only on demand via read_output offset. Tasks: real sto fix tasks touching Go/TS/Python (top 3 repo langs) across eka-cli, eka-core, eka-mcp. Metrics: tokens_transmitted, success on fix task (tests pass, correct symbol edited), noise metric (irrelevant lines sent), precision@k for locating change site.\n\nPrototype approach: minimal tree-sitter per file (Go/TS/Python) + import graph from import/require statements + 1-hop callee name match, no full call graph, no vector. Cache per fileHash, invalidation per gitDigest — measure indexing cost and determinism. Also sketch fallback for dynamic langs (Python/JS) incompleteness. Timebox 5 days. Deliverable: precision @k, token delta, indexing cost, and GO/NO-GO for code_context MVP (file map + import graph + symbol index). If GO, defines bounded closure 32/64 and --no-content analogue for code.","conclusion":""}